```java
public class Solution {
    public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        while (n % 3 == 0 && n != 0) {
            n /= 3;
        }
        return n == 1;
    }
}
```

This function works by repeatedly dividing `n` by 3 until it becomes equal to 1 or less than 3. If at any point `n` is not equal to 1, then it means that `n` is not a power of three and the function returns `false`. If `n` eventually becomes equal to 1, then it means that `n` was originally a power of three and the function returns `true`.

The time complexity of this function is O(log(n)), since we divide `n` by 3 in each iteration until it reaches 1. The space complexity is also O(1), as we only use a constant amount of extra space for storing the intermediate value `n`.

To create a LATEX document that displays a chessboard using FEN notation, we need to use the `fen` package along with some additional packages such as `tikz` for drawing the board. Here's an example:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{fen}

% Define the FEN notation for a standard 8x8 chessboard
\newcommand{\fen}[1]{\textcolor{red}{#1}}

% Draw the FEN notation for a standard 8x8 chessboard
\begin{document}
\begin{center}
    \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
        % Draw the top row
        \foreach \i in {1,...,8} {
            \node at (0, #i) {\fen{$i$}};
        }

        % Draw the middle row
        \foreach \i in {9,...,16} {
            \node at (2, #i) {\fen{$i$}};
        }

        % Draw the bottom row
        \foreach \i in {17,...,24} {
            \node at (3, #i) {\fen{$i$}};
        }
    \end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}
\end{document}
```

This code defines a new command `\fen` which takes a number as input and returns its corresponding FEN notation. It then uses TikZ to draw the FEN notation for a standard 8x8 chessboard.

